#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "11d9077c-a0ac-5d42-9616-b8fe867d1db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9076" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ffaf242238a8d32ce0f134cff6e0cf4256b2888b0a71f65e3c5ec470cbce284a.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}, \\: e^{y/3} + 3x \\right\\rangle.$ \nFind a repeated integral in plane polar coordinates that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + \\left( e^{y/3} + 3x \\right) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e4/e4c7913793a4b08916a832e575c5b2c167d5dfeec244eea23857731df1e514e7.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= 2y + \\ln{(\\sqrt{x+5})}$ and $Q(x,y)=e^{y/3} + 3x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x+5}))\\,dx+({e}^{y/3}+3x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 3, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175878 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176023 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2eca638-7c7b-51ed-99e3-3b3156d13669" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "1ee74b2f-adff-5c38-8b68-753b1c02f91c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9550" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/f1/f16fa431ecef10f5538168a49f4f3ceb4ec827114cab6854142fc9ca4d9ce9af.svg) where the path ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is shown above." :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/ac/accf185e064f4c9c99cc6ad1437aabc7d5471cc332ff6c38d51a3f11ec6e26f2.svg)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) and its boundary ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/8c/8cbe1833f3c6f1612d69aecc8354e66cbf24ef426d648ed5c90224bbcf5ab341.svg)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/01/01e3a66875647bfc9704bc6a1066dbc18c5cf1c0b0144aff8eb2e478685f83d2.svg)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/49/493f02d8a665f5b2dedf5ab543475a28b82bc393d3290a60250d26fbb8065b01.svg)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/2c/2c2141d090d129fde56fdfd334e4162927db247703b7ecd62eb14261340ac929.svg)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/19/19dfe81d2ed118fd87e4e0ac483119e68f4bbf5ec67ccbaf2614360015ff193a.svg)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c7d035d915ae4a4cc993d476824897130b85797f32ad753f86e1accd1d5e29e.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/1e/1e78bf74df5607a1d80b3bd51b3222505e3bb201817448a7a5ebe5b0a73cffa9.svg)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6afcc73d-a8a6-5615-9d7c-fa0873dda7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}2r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242139} {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2aeae-ff81-519e-b9b7-d9f8716c6540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242523} {:answer/id #uuid "bc85f820-b7f6-5517-a066-986ad9b00f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242832} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a5a0d5-6ff6-5128-b57e-53fc5760b7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}({r}^{3}-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87e2b68d-147e-555d-9b7c-88c63841a64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242371} :answer-field/id #uuid "488d900f-29ce-5ad3-9267-3a76fc467aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6e3c388-6148-5c9d-8b7f-1f6ea60657b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-108544" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0964ff8219d84181163537f5660fe2b504668b1e97e3461cc8cb43d18fc6091.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/de/deeb5648e328ecc93cf6c1de620e103da0f7677705d1e89e4654deff8d411f66.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=7x+\\sqrt{y+5},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3y-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dx+(7x+\\sqrt{y+5})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9211e1af-2c66-5f2d-9047-11aa8e63b870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{3}\\sin 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174125} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d2e78d-20ab-5f92-81f4-4061dc11ec75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\cos θ} - \\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174126} {:answer/id #uuid "029cc37d-1300-55e1-a1eb-c0396c774c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174127} {:answer/id #uuid "1f508d93-fe49-5796-a8ee-e2f2e93f9e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r + r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174128} {:answer/id #uuid "63a45ad0-78f6-598a-9b00-e8310310981b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "re^{\\cos θ} - r\\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174129}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9608a74f-b086-5f31-b192-ae95683f2fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174124}] :question/id #uuid "e77d34a7-9f25-5206-895c-5f4fa0d1f689" :question/math-academy-id "q-125716" :question/problem "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}AAAAAAAAdrdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨xy^{2}, x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ad61f7-0826-5aab-ae4e-a6c43c772071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416081} {:answer/id #uuid "5a99ee20-4856-5c51-8e4b-86d8dbf5a614" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416084} {:answer/id #uuid "a973df46-4a12-5e49-ab49-9f61bef424b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416097} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e1769-fccf-57f9-b3d1-36eb4847c17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416101} {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3419ad6-a21f-5e70-a985-87306ae9808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416102} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ef8539-57c5-5120-9995-c808fd171d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdb21538-b802-59ae-83c3-94940f8f1068" :question/math-academy-id "q-126516" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc0660c37a6658635311a9a973ae3f4802740622a0b35ade22d6050ce648efa6.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/77/7724df0035511419dcb5117ba8aa337380325af22ee235d206a954aa12c62fe9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}-x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}-x)\\,dx+({x}^{3}+{e}^{1/y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}3({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458686} {:answer/id #uuid "648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458801} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} {:answer/id #uuid "76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458837} {:answer/id #uuid "867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/math-academy-id "q-126532" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017f8f24-8529-55cc-a1a8-41dc999d12f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0e048-b5e5-51cc-8ee4-8a54d22a60bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405046} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65de8-c1f0-596d-875a-4aa5f454bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047} {:answer/id #uuid "0592c862-0b50-54dc-acf8-8691c79146ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405048} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c51a5c2-f91d-5c41-ab32-882df0c70ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416077} {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5c6d03-4f8a-5ba0-9230-947fbebb29b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}({r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416082} {:answer/id #uuid "89460c96-8227-5b1e-b670-b297b665d081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416088} {:answer/id #uuid "a650e27c-da32-56b0-88b1-5dd26bdd90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35da7698-acba-5a15-9327-eb06db3b24c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416078} :answer-field/id #uuid "80955fcf-3708-5539-a929-c138089951ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06de88f2-295c-546b-af7c-8eedcb3a386b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126708" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e1/e1c053e1cc4b4304c1302da2c57278200931b84e4ba687269d0ea89a99b523b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(-xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}(-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5704a2-acbe-5ccb-98dc-aa77e0ccd803" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088226e5-e75c-5742-95e5-cf251dc2c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259954} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6c965-52e9-544a-b893-b0aec4e0ed51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260702} {:answer/id #uuid "68106dca-1a59-5004-9754-bd47cf8b89f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260783} {:answer/id #uuid "d83492fb-fd44-5225-bf11-33dcf491c32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261779} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74461d-0ba1-5d66-a17d-00ce3adbc2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83492fb-fd44-5225-bf11-33dcf491c32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd0d439-6074-5bac-8272-423170c14f7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b7ce0c4-9186-5034-8ec0-50be4181952e" :question/math-academy-id "q-126711" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cb15d895d5baf46d359d811fce99b73238434eb01079d1199cfd99d3f52f222.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}+{x}^{2},\\,xy+y{e}^{y}⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/23/232ced663dcac27ce2775af66dd2ef215ed937e0f4eb5e3e6fb008a3d82dc069.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+y{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,dx+(xy+y{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
